chx:Document a doc:ReportSection ; rdfs:label "Dictionaries for an advanced course: chapter 7 of the 3rd edition and today's Python"@en ; skos:definition "This document renews chapter 7 of the 3rd edition for an advanced course (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07/research> ;
    doc:hasSection chx:S_DictionaryBasics, chx:S_ReadingAndChanging, chx:S_ViewsAndLoops, chx:S_CountingAndGrouping, chx:S_StructuringData, chx:S_PracticeWork .
chx:S_DictionaryBasics a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Dictionary basics"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Dictionary basics" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 1 ;
    skos:definition "The dictionary type, the keys that index it and the way two dictionaries compare are the base of Chapter 7 (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#DictionaryBasics> .
chx:S_Structure a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Structure"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Structure" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 2 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_DictionaryBasics ;
    skos:definition "The chapter starts from the dictionary value written between curly brackets and from the ways Python offers to build one (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Structure> .
chx:S_DictionaryType a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Dictionary type"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Dictionary type" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 3 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Structure ;
    skos:definition "my_cat = {'size': 'fat', 'color': 'gray', 'age': 17} has the keys 'size', 'color' and 'age', and my_cat['size'] is 'fat' and 'My cat has ' + my_cat['color'] + ' fur.' is 'My cat has gray fur.' (Sweigart, 2025); the empty dictionary is {} and is of type dict, while {'a'} is a set because an empty pair of braces cannot also be an empty set, as executed for this study (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#DictionaryType> .
chx:S_DictionaryConstructors a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Dictionary constructors"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Dictionary constructors" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 4 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Structure ;
    skos:definition "The chapter enters every dictionary as a literal; the dict constructor also accepts keyword arguments, pairs, another mapping and zip of two lists, so that dict(one=1, two=2, three=3), dict(zip(['one', 'two', 'three'], [1, 2, 3])) and dict([('two', 2), ('one', 1), ('three', 3)]) are all equal to {'one': 1, 'two': 2, 'three': 3}, keyword arguments work only for keys that are valid names, and dict([('k', 1), ('k', 2)]) is {'k': 2} because the last value for a repeated key wins; the chapter as read does not present the constructor (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#DictionaryConstructors> .
chx:S_Keys a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Keys"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Keys" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 5 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_DictionaryBasics ;
    skos:definition "A dictionary is read through its keys, which must be hashable and which may be of many types (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Keys> .
chx:S_KeysNotIndexes a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Keys not indexes"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Keys not indexes" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 6 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Keys ;
    skos:definition "With spam = {12345: 'Luggage Combination', 42: 'The Answer'}, spam[12345] is 'Luggage Combination' and spam[42] is 'The Answer', while spam[0] raises KeyError: 0, because the dictionary has keys and no index 0 through 41, and a missing 'color' key raises KeyError: 'color' whose message is the repr of the key (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#KeysNotIndexes> .
chx:S_HashableKeys a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Hashable keys"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Hashable keys" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 7 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Keys ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that a list or a dictionary cannot be a key because these types are unhashable, and that a tuple should be used where a list is wanted (Sweigart, 2025); under 3.14.4 {[1, 2]: 'x'} raises TypeError: cannot use 'list' as a dict key (unhashable type: 'list') and the same for a dictionary, a tuple such as (2, 3) works as a key, a tuple holding a list as in (1, [2]) is refused with TypeError: cannot use 'tuple' as a dict key (unhashable type: 'list'), and a class that defines __eq__ and no __hash__ is unhashable too, the rule being that a key's hash must not change while it is in the dictionary (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#HashableKeys> .
chx:S_EqualKeysShareASlot a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Equal keys share a slot"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Equal keys share a slot" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 8 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Keys ;
    skos:definition "{1: 'a', 1.0: 'b', True: 'c'} is {1: 'c'}, because 1, 1.0 and True are equal and have the same hash, so the later assignments replace the value and the first key object, an int, stays; the chapter as read does not present this (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#EqualKeysShareASlot> .
chx:S_Comparison a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Comparison"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Comparison" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 9 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_DictionaryBasics ;
    skos:definition "The chapter compares dictionaries with lists by equality and by order (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Comparison> .
chx:S_OrderInsensitiveEquality a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Order insensitive equality"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Order insensitive equality" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 10 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Comparison ;
    skos:definition "['cats', 'dogs', 'moose'] == ['dogs', 'moose', 'cats'] is False but eggs == ham is True for {'name': 'Zophie', 'species': 'cat', 'age': '8'} and the same pairs entered in another order, although list(eggs) and list(ham) differ, so equality of dictionaries ignores the order while the order of the keys in each is kept (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#OrderInsensitiveEquality> .
chx:S_InsertionOrder a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Insertion order"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Insertion order" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 11 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Comparison ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that items in dictionaries are unordered and that there is no first item (Sweigart, 2025); under Python 3.14.4 list({'one': 1, 'two': 2, 'three': 3, 'four': 4}) is ['one', 'two', 'three', 'four'], after d['one'] = 42, del d['two'] and d['two'] = None the dictionary is {'one': 42, 'three': 3, 'four': 4, 'two': None}, so a replaced value keeps its place and a re-added key goes last, and the documentation guarantees this order since Python 3.7 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#InsertionOrder> .
chx:S_MappingNotSequence a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Mapping not sequence"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Mapping not sequence" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 12 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Comparison ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that, because a dictionary is not ordered, it is not a sequence and cannot be sliced (Sweigart, 2025); under 3.14.4 d[0:2] raises KeyError: slice(0, 2, None), since a slice is a hashable key since Python 3.12, {'a': 1} < {'a': 2} raises TypeError: '<' not supported between instances of 'dict' and 'dict', isinstance(d, Mapping) is True and isinstance(d, Sequence) is False, and list(reversed(d)) is ['two', 'four', 'three', 'one'] for the dictionary above, reversed order of insertion since Python 3.8 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#MappingNotSequence> .
chx:S_ReadingAndChanging a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Reading and changing"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Reading and changing" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 13 ;
    skos:definition "Reading a value, testing for a key and changing the pairs of a dictionary are the operations the chapter builds on the dictionary type (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#ReadingAndChanging> .
chx:S_Reading a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Reading"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Reading" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 14 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ReadingAndChanging ;
    skos:definition "A key is tested with in, read with a subscript or with get(), and ensured with setdefault() (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Reading> .
chx:S_MembershipOnKeys a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Membership on keys"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Membership on keys" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 15 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Reading ;
    skos:definition "'color' in spam and 'color' in spam.keys() are both True for {'color': 'red', 'age': 42}, 'age' not in spam.keys() is False, 'red' in spam.values() is True and 'red' in spam alone is False, because the operators on the dictionary itself test keys (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#MembershipOnKeys> .
chx:S_GetMethod a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Get method"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Get method" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 16 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Reading ;
    skos:definition "picnic_items.get('cups', 0) is 2 and picnic_items.get('eggs', 0) is 0 for {'apples': 5, 'cups': 2}, so the chapter's sentences I am bringing 2 cups. and I am bringing 0 eggs. come out, and picnic_items['eggs'] raises KeyError: 'eggs' (Sweigart, 2025); get without a fallback gives None and never adds the key to the dictionary (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#GetMethod> .
chx:S_SetdefaultMethod a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Setdefault method"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Setdefault method" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 17 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Reading ;
    skos:definition "spam.setdefault('color', 'black') on {'name': 'Pooka', 'age': 5} returns 'black' and leaves {'name': 'Pooka', 'age': 5, 'color': 'black'}, and spam.setdefault('color', 'white') returns 'black' and changes nothing (Sweigart, 2025); groups.setdefault('even', []).append(2) followed by the same with 4 and with 'odd' and 1 gives {'even': [2, 4], 'odd': [1]} (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#SetdefaultMethod> .
chx:S_Changing a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Changing"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Changing" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 18 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ReadingAndChanging ;
    skos:definition "A dictionary changes in the same object by assignment to a key, by del, by pop and popitem, by update and by the merge operators (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Changing> .
chx:S_AddOrReplacePair a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Add or replace pair"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Add or replace pair" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 19 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Changing ;
    skos:definition "The birthdays program stores a new birthday with birthdays[name] = bday, which adds the key 'Eve' with 'Dec 5' and would replace the value of an existing name, and the chapter notes that everything entered is forgotten when the program ends (Sweigart, 2025); assigning to an existing key keeps its place in the order (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#AddOrReplacePair> .
chx:S_DeletePair a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Delete pair"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Delete pair" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 20 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Changing ;
    skos:definition "The chessboard program's remove command is del main_board[response[1]], which deletes the pair of a square so that the piece disappears; under 3.14.4 del d['a'] on {'a': 1, 'b': 2, 'c': 3, 'd': 4} leaves {'b': 2, 'c': 3, 'd': 4}, del d['zz'] raises KeyError: 'zz', and clear() leaves {} (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#DeletePair> .
chx:S_PopMethod a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Pop method"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Pop method" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 21 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Changing ;
    skos:definition "pop() is not in the chapter as read; under 3.14.4 d.pop('b') returns 2 and removes the pair, d.pop('zz', 'absent') returns 'absent', d.pop('zz') raises KeyError: 'zz', and d.popitem() returns the last pair inserted, ('d', 4), and on an empty dictionary raises KeyError: 'popitem(): dictionary is empty', the last-in first-out order being guaranteed since Python 3.7 (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#PopMethod> .
chx:S_UpdateAndMerge a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Update and merge"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Update and merge" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 22 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Changing ;
    skos:definition "update() is not in the chapter as read; under 3.14.4 d.update({'b': 20, 'c': 3}, d=4) returns None and gives {'a': 1, 'b': 20, 'c': 3, 'd': 4}, a | b gives {'x': 1, 'y': 20, 'z': 3} from {'x': 1, 'y': 2} and {'y': 20, 'z': 3} and leaves a at {'x': 1, 'y': 2}, a |= b changes a itself, d |= [('b', 2)] accepts pairs while {'a': 1} | [('b', 2)] raises TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for |: 'dict' and 'list', and {**{'x': 1, 'y': 2}, **{'y': 20}} is {'x': 1, 'y': 20} (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (D'Aprano and Bucher, 2019) (Landau, 2013)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#UpdateAndMerge> .
chx:S_FromkeysMethod a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Fromkeys method"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Fromkeys method" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 23 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Changing ;
    skos:definition "dict.fromkeys('abc', 0) is {'a': 0, 'b': 0, 'c': 0}, but dict.fromkeys('ab', []) gives both keys the same list, so appending 1 through one key shows it under both as {'a': [1], 'b': [1]}, while {k: [] for k in 'ab'} gives each key its own list; the method is not in the chapter as read (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#FromkeysMethod> .
chx:S_FirstProgram a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "First program"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "First program" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 24 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ReadingAndChanging ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's first program keeps a dictionary of birthdays between questions (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#FirstProgram> .
chx:S_BirthdayLookup a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Birthday lookup"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Birthday lookup" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 25 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_FirstProgram ;
    skos:definition "birthdays.py holds {'Alice': 'Apr 1', 'Bob': 'Dec 12', 'Carol': 'Mar 4'}, tests a typed name with in, prints birthdays[name] or asks for a birthday and stores it, and with the answers Alice, Eve, Dec 5, Eve and a blank line the program printed exactly the chapter's exchange, ending with Dec 5 is the birthday of Eve and a last prompt (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#BirthdayLookup> .
chx:S_ViewsAndLoops a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Views and loops"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Views and loops" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 26 ;
    skos:definition "The views of a dictionary and the loops over it are the chapter's second topic, together with the comprehension that builds one (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#ViewsAndLoops> .
chx:S_Views a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Views"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Views" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 27 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ViewsAndLoops ;
    skos:definition "The methods keys(), values() and items() return view objects (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Views> .
chx:S_KeysValuesItems a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Keys values items"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Keys values items" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 28 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Views ;
    skos:definition "For {'color': 'red', 'age': 42}, spam.keys() prints dict_keys(['color', 'age']), spam.values() prints dict_values(['red', 42]), spam.items() prints dict_items([('color', 'red'), ('age', 42)]) and the items are tuples, list(spam.keys()) is ['color', 'age'], and spam.keys().append('x') raises AttributeError: 'dict_keys' object has no attribute 'append' while spam.keys()[0] raises TypeError: 'dict_keys' object is not subscriptable (Sweigart, 2025); the chapter calls the views list-like values that cannot be modified (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#KeysValuesItems> .
chx:S_LiveAndSetLikeViews a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Live and set like views"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Live and set like views" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 29 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Views ;
    skos:definition "A view follows the dictionary: after keys = dishes.keys() and the deletion of two entries, list(keys) is ['bacon', 'spam'] and list(values) is [1, 500], values.mapping is a read-only view of the dictionary, keys & {'eggs', 'bacon', 'salad'} is {'bacon'}, {'a': 1}.items() <= {'a': 1, 'b': 2}.items() is True, and d.values() == d.values() is False because values views are never equal; the chapter as read does not present this (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#LiveAndSetLikeViews> .
chx:S_Looping a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Looping"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Looping" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 30 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ViewsAndLoops ;
    skos:definition "A for loop over a dictionary takes the keys, the values or the pairs, in insertion order (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Looping> .
chx:S_ItemsLoop a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Items loop"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Items loop" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 31 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Looping ;
    skos:definition "for k, v in spam.items() assigns the key and the value of each pair to two names, printing Key: color Value: red and Key: age Value: 42, because each item of the view is a tuple and the loop uses multiple assignment (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#ItemsLoop> .
chx:S_SortedAndReversedLoops a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Sorted and reversed loops"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Sorted and reversed loops" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 32 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Looping ;
    skos:definition "For d = {'b': 1, 'c': 3, 'a': 2}, sorted(d) is ['a', 'b', 'c'], list(reversed(d)) is ['a', 'c', 'b'], sorted(d.items(), key=lambda p: p[1]) is [('b', 1), ('a', 2), ('c', 3)] and sorted(d, key=d.get, reverse=True) is ['c', 'a', 'b']; the chapter as read does not present sorting a dictionary (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#SortedAndReversedLoops> .
chx:S_ChangeWhileLooping a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Change while looping"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Change while looping" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 33 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Looping ;
    skos:definition "Adding keys inside for k in d: d[k + 'x'] = 0 raises RuntimeError: dictionary changed size during iteration, deleting over list(d) leaves {'c': 3} from {'b': 1, 'c': 3, 'a': 2} when values below 3 are removed, and assigning new values to existing keys during the loop is allowed, turning the values into 10, 30 and 20; the chapter as read does not present the trap (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#ChangeWhileLooping> .
chx:S_Building a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Building"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Building" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 34 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ViewsAndLoops ;
    skos:definition "A dictionary can be built by a loop, by the constructor or by a comprehension (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Building> .
chx:S_DictComprehension a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Dict comprehension"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Dict comprehension" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 35 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Building ;
    skos:definition "{x: x**2 for x in (2, 4, 6)} is {2: 4, 4: 16, 6: 36}, {v: k for k, v in prices.items()} inverts {'apple': 3, 'pear': 5, 'fig': 9} into {3: 'apple', 5: 'pear', 9: 'fig'}, and an if clause keeps {'pear': 5, 'fig': 9}; the chapter as read does not present dictionary comprehensions (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Warsaw, 2001)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#DictComprehension> .
chx:S_CountingAndGrouping a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Counting and grouping"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Counting and grouping" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 36 ;
    skos:definition "Counting occurrences and grouping values under keys are the jobs the chapter's setdefault method is shown for, and the standard library has tools for both (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#CountingAndGrouping> .
chx:S_Counting a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Counting"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Counting" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 37 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_CountingAndGrouping ;
    skos:definition "A dictionary of counts maps each item to the number of times it occurred (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Counting> .
chx:S_CharacterCount a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Character count"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Character count" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 38 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Counting ;
    skos:definition "characterCount.py counts the characters of 'It was a bright cold day in April, and the clocks were striking thirteen.' with count.setdefault(character, 0) and count[character] = count[character] + 1, and under 3.14.4 it printed the dictionary the chapter prints, equal to it and with its keys in the same order, with 'c' three times, the space 13 times and 'A' once (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#CharacterCount> .
chx:S_CounterClass a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Counter class"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Counter class" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 39 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Counting ;
    skos:definition "Counter(message) gave a dictionary equal to the hand-made count of characterCount, Counter is a subclass of dict, Counter(message).most_common(3) is [(' ', 13), ('t', 6), ('i', 6)], and Counter('abc')['z'] is 0 without adding 'z'; the chapter as read does not present Counter (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#CounterClass> .
chx:S_DefaultdictCounting a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Defaultdict counting"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Defaultdict counting" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 40 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Counting ;
    skos:definition "defaultdict(int) with d[ch] += 1 for each character of the message gave a dictionary equal to the same count, because a missing key is filled with int(), which is 0; the chapter as read does not present defaultdict (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#DefaultdictCounting> .
chx:S_Grouping a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Grouping"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Grouping" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 41 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_CountingAndGrouping ;
    skos:definition "Grouping keeps a list of values for each key (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Grouping> .
chx:S_GroupingIntoLists a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Grouping into lists"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Grouping into lists" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 42 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Grouping ;
    skos:definition "For the pairs [('yellow', 1), ('blue', 2), ('yellow', 3), ('blue', 4), ('red', 1)], g = defaultdict(list) with g[k].append(v) gives sorted(g.items()) equal to [('blue', [2, 4]), ('red', [1]), ('yellow', [1, 3])], and d.setdefault(k, []).append(v) on a plain dictionary groups the same way (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#GroupingIntoLists> .
chx:S_MissingKeyHook a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Missing key hook"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Missing key hook" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 43 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Grouping ;
    skos:definition "dd = defaultdict(list) gives dd['x'] as [] and then 'x' in dd is True, but dd.get('y') is None and 'y' in dd is False, because only a subscript calls __missing__, and defaultdict() with no factory raises KeyError: 'k' like a plain dictionary (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#MissingKeyHook> .
chx:S_OrderedDictType a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Ordered dict type"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Ordered dict type" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 44 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Grouping ;
    skos:definition "OrderedDict([('a', 1), ('b', 2)]) == OrderedDict([('b', 2), ('a', 1)]) is False where the same two plain dictionaries are equal, an OrderedDict equals a plain dictionary whatever the order, move_to_end('a') put 'a' last giving ['b', 'a'], and ChainMap(overrides, defaults) looked up 'user' as 'admin' and 'color' as 'red'; the chapter as read does not present them (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#OrderedDictType> .
chx:S_StructuringData a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Structuring data"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Structuring data" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 45 ;
    skos:definition "Modelling a real-world thing with nested dictionaries and lists is the chapter's second half (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#StructuringData> .
chx:S_Modelling a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Modelling"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Modelling" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 46 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StructuringData ;
    skos:definition "The interactive chessboard shows how a data structure is chosen first and the functions written to fit it (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Modelling> .
chx:S_ChessboardModel a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Chessboard model"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Chessboard model" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 47 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modelling ;
    skos:definition "The board is a dictionary with keys 'a1' to 'h8' and two-character values such as 'wK' and 'bQ', the absence of a key meaning an empty square, and {'h1': 'bK', 'c6': 'wQ', 'g2': 'bB', 'h5': 'bQ', 'e3': 'wK'} has 5 pieces, no piece on 'd4' and one on 'e3'; STARTING_PIECES as typed holds 32 pieces, 16 white and 16 black (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#ChessboardModel> .
chx:S_ChessboardPrinter a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Chessboard printer"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Chessboard printer" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 48 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modelling ;
    skos:definition "print_chessboard(board) loops y over '87654321' and x over 'abcdefgh', so the squares come in the order a8, b8 and on to h8 and then a7, which is left to right although the chapter's text says right to left, appends the piece or the white or black empty-square string, flips is_white_square after each square and again after each row, and prints a template with 64 pairs of braces over 27 lines, which for the five-piece board printed the text board with wQ on c6, bQ on h5, wK on e3, bB on g2 and bK on h1 (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#ChessboardPrinter> .
chx:S_StarSyntax a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Star syntax"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Star syntax" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 49 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modelling ;
    skos:definition "BOARD_TEMPLATE.format(*squares) passes the 64 strings of the list as 64 arguments, print(*['cat', 'dog', 'rat']) prints cat dog rat, '{}-{}-{}'.format(*['cat', 'dog', 'rat']) is 'cat-dog-rat', and '{} {} {}'.format(['cat', 'dog', 'rat']) without the star raises IndexError: Replacement index 1 out of range for positional args tuple (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#StarSyntax> .
chx:S_ChessboardCommands a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Chessboard commands"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Chessboard commands" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 50 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Modelling ;
    skos:definition "The simulator splits the typed line, 'move e2 e4'.split() being ['move', 'e2', 'e4'], and acts by dictionary operations: move assigns the piece to the new key and deletes the old, remove deletes, set assigns, reset copies STARTING_PIECES, clear rebinds an empty dictionary, fill assigns a piece to all 64 keys and quit calls sys.exit(); with the command handling run on lines instead of input(), move e2 e4 left 32 pieces with e4 holding wP, fill wP gave 64 squares of 'wP', reset gave a board equal to but not the same object as STARTING_PIECES, and move e3 e4 and remove e3 on the starting board raised KeyError: 'e3', move e2 raised IndexError: list index out of range, and move e2 e7 captured the pawn on e7 and left 31 pieces, since the program checks nothing (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#ChessboardCommands> .
chx:S_Nesting a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Nesting"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Nesting" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 51 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StructuringData ;
    skos:definition "A dictionary value may itself be a dictionary or a list (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Nesting> .
chx:S_NestedDictionaries a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Nested dictionaries"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Nested dictionaries" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 52 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Nesting ;
    skos:definition "all_guests = {'Alice': {'apples': 5, 'pretzels': 12}, 'Bob': {'ham sandwiches': 3, 'apples': 2}, 'Carol': {'cups': 3, 'apple pies': 1}} holds a dictionary of items for each guest, and all_guests['Alice']['apples'] is 5 by two subscripts while all_guests['Bob'].get('cups', 0) is 0 (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#NestedDictionaries> .
chx:S_TotalBrought a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Total brought"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Total brought" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 53 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Nesting ;
    skos:definition "total_brought(guests, item) loops over guests.items(), adds v.get(item, 0) for each guest and printed Number of things being brought: with Apples 7, Cups 3, Cakes 0, Ham Sandwiches 3 and Apple Pies 1, as the chapter prints (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#TotalBrought> .
chx:S_CopyingDictionaries a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Copying dictionaries"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Copying dictionaries" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 54 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Nesting ;
    skos:definition "copy.copy(STARTING_PIECES) gives the board a copy it can change, so moving e2 to e4 on the copy left the starting dictionary at its two pieces, and STARTING.copy() == STARTING with STARTING.copy() is STARTING False and dict(STARTING) another new dictionary; for a nested dictionary d.copy() shares the inner dictionary, so after nested['a']['x'] = 9 the shallow copy shows {'a': {'x': 9}} and copy.deepcopy shows {'a': {'x': 1}} (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#CopyingDictionaries> .
chx:S_Notation a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Notation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Notation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 55 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_StructuringData ;
    skos:definition "Nested data can be shown, typed, written out and taken apart with the standard library, beyond the chapter (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Notation> .
chx:S_PrettyPrinting a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Pretty printing"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Pretty printing" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 56 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Notation ;
    skos:definition "Question 8 asks for the module and function that pretty-print a dictionary, which is pprint and pprint.pprint; with width=40 pprint.pprint printed a nested dictionary over several lines with the keys in sorted order and pprint.pp in insertion order, pformat({'b': 1, 'a': 2}) returns the text {'a': 2, 'b': 1} and with sort_dicts=False the text {'b': 1, 'a': 2}, and json.dumps with sort_keys=True writes the nested dictionary as text, json.loads reads it back and a tuple key raises TypeError: keys must be str, int, float, bool or None, not tuple; the chapter as read does not teach pprint or json (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#PrettyPrinting> .
chx:S_TypedModels a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Typed models"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Typed models" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 57 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Notation ;
    skos:definition "A class Piece(TypedDict) with color and kind fields gave Piece(color='w', kind='K') equal to the plain dictionary {'color': 'w', 'kind': 'K'} of type dict, with its types in Piece.__annotations__, so nothing is checked at run time; a dataclass Square('e', 4) prints Square(file='e', rank=4), and MappingProxyType({'a': 1}) gives a view that can be read but not assigned to, raising TypeError: 'mappingproxy' object does not support item assignment; the chapter as read does not present them (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Lehtosalo, 2019)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#TypedModels> .
chx:S_MappingPattern a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Mapping pattern"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Mapping pattern" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 58 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Notation ;
    skos:definition "In a match statement the pattern {'op': 'move', 'from': a, 'to': b} matched {'op': 'move', 'from': 'e2', 'to': 'e4', 'extra': 1} and bound the two squares, {'op': str(o), **rest} collected the remaining keys, giving unknown fill with ['piece'], and a list matched none of them; the chapter as read does not present the match statement (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Bucher and van Rossum, 2020)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#MappingPattern> .
chx:S_PracticeWork a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Practice work"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Practice work" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 59 ;
    skos:definition "The chapter closes with 8 practice questions and 3 practice programs (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#PracticeWork> .
chx:S_Questions a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Questions"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Questions" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 60 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PracticeWork ;
    skos:definition "The practice questions are answered by the dictionary operations of the chapter (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Questions> .
chx:S_PracticeQuestions a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Practice questions"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Practice questions" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 61 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Questions ;
    skos:definition "Run for this study, question 1 is {}, question 2 is {'foo': 42}, question 4 raises KeyError: 'foo' for spam['foo'] with spam = {'bar': 100}, in spam and in spam.keys() are both True for a key and only in spam.values() tests values, the shortcut of question 7 is spam.setdefault('color', 'black') which gave the same dictionary as the if test, and question 8, pprint.pformat({'b': 2, 'a': 1}), returns the text {'a': 1, 'b': 2} (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#PracticeQuestions> .
chx:S_Programs a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Programs"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Programs" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 62 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PracticeWork ;
    skos:definition "The three practice programs apply the dictionary to a chessboard check, a game inventory and a list of loot (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#Programs> .
chx:S_ChessValidator a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Chess validator"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Chess validator" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 63 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Programs ;
    skos:definition "A validator written for this study for the chapter's own notation accepted the 32-piece starting position and the five-piece board, and refused a board with the square 'z9', one with no white king, one with the piece 'wX' and one with 9 white pawns; the practice program itself speaks of squares from '1a' to '8h' and of pieces named 'pawn' or 'knight', which differs from the chapter's 'a1' and 'wP' (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#ChessValidator> .
chx:S_FantasyInventory a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Fantasy inventory"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Fantasy inventory" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 64 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Programs ;
    skos:definition "display_inventory(stuff) for {'rope': 1, 'torch': 6, 'gold coin': 42, 'dagger': 1, 'arrow': 12} printed Inventory:, the lines 1 rope, 6 torch, 42 gold coin, 1 dagger and 12 arrow in insertion order, and Total number of items: 62, where the chapter's sample lists 12 arrow first, an order a loop over items() does not give (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#FantasyInventory> .
chx:S_LootConversion a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Loot conversion"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Loot conversion" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 65 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Programs ;
    skos:definition "add_to_inventory(inv, dragon_loot) with inventory.get(item, 0) + 1 for each item turned {'gold coin': 42, 'rope': 1} and ['gold coin', 'dagger', 'gold coin', 'gold coin', 'ruby'] into gold coin 45, rope 1, dagger 1 and ruby 1, a total of 48, where the chapter's sample lists ruby before dagger and a blank line before the total (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch07#LootConversion> .
